<h1>Anaxagoras</h1><div id="pubinfo"><em>First published Wed Aug 22, 2007; substantive revision Sun Aug 9, 2026</em></div>

<div id="preamble">

<p>
When [Anaxagoras] said that <em>no&ucirc;s</em> is present &mdash; in
nature just as it is in animals &mdash; as the cause of the cosmos and
of all its order, he appeared as a sober man among the random
chatterers who preceded him. (Aristotle, <em>Metaphysics</em> I.3
984b15)</p>

<p>
Anaxagoras of Clazomenae (500&ndash;428 BCE) is an important figure in
the history of physics and cosmology. He is the earliest philosopher
to have resided in Athens. He used empirical evidence to guide the
development of his cosmology and science. He was the first Greek
theorist to offer the correct account of eclipses. He was also the
first to posit that solid heavy bodies exist in the heavens. Further,
he thought that these bodies are held aloft by a violent vortex and he
claimed that, overcoming this force, some of these bodies would
ultimately fall and crash down to Earth. Within just a few years of
his first advancing this theory, a stone &ldquo;the size of a
wagon&rdquo; (Diels-Kranz [DK] 59A11: translations for Anaxagoras are
based on those in Curd 2007) crashed to the ground at Aegospotami.
Overnight, Anaxagoras gained notoriety for having
&lsquo;predicted&rsquo; the fall of the meteorite. He also introduced
the idea that space is infinitely extended and infinitely divisible.
He is praised as &ldquo;the most brilliant scientist in
antiquity&rdquo; (Janko 2020, 213) and &ldquo;the greatest astronomer
of the early 5<sup>th</sup> century&rdquo; (Graham 2013c, 158). In
addition, he is celebrated as &ldquo;the only Greek physicist who made
physics completely congruent with mathematics&rdquo; (Arsenijevic et
al. 2019, 59).</p>

<p>
Anaxagoras&rsquo; physics rests on five core principles which are
developed in response to the theory of metaphysical monism that is
advanced by Parmenides of Elea (515&ndash;450 BCE). Anaxagoras&rsquo;
principles are: <em>No Becoming</em>, <em>Universal Mixture</em>,
<em>Indefinite Types</em>, <em>Thorough Infinitism</em>, and
<em>Predominance</em>. Aristotle suggests that Anaxagoras also
embraced the principle of <em>Homoiomereity</em>. Anaxagoras advances
his treatment of physics and cosmology in two stages. First, he
describes a spatially unlimited universe, which is a dense and
void-less expanse that is filled with a mixture of pre-existing and
interpenetrating types of matter. The mix is so thorough that no
phenomenally distinct objects are evident. Anaxagoras affirms that
<em>no&ucirc;s</em> (mind, reason, sense, wit) is coextensive with, or
is in some manner fully dispersed throughout, the plenum. Second, he
claims that <em>no&ucirc;s</em> is responsible for a vortex which
surges outward from a small region in the plenum. The vortex expands,
bringing about a redistribution of matter, and this redistribution
causes the emergence of non-basic, but phenomenally distinct, physical
objects. Ongoing redistribution also causes the destruction of
non-basic objects. The <em>no&ucirc;s</em>-caused vortex produces our
structured world, and it produces innumerable other worlds that are
isomorphic with our own.</p>

<p>
Anaxagoras theorizes about a variety of astronomical, meteorological,
and geological phenomena, including eclipses, shooting stars, comets,
meteorites, hail, and the flooding of the Nile. Regarding his
influence on later philosophers, Anaxagoras&rsquo; treatment of
<em>no&ucirc;s</em> has proven to be the most impactful element of his
overall theory.</p>
</div> 

